WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Two, four, six pass. Must the rule be even numbers?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Another explanation may fit just as well.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
In 1960, Wason studied numerical rule discovery.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Participants submitted triples and received feedback.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
Similar positive cases can leave several hypotheses open.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
“All even” accepts two, four, six.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
“Increasing” accepts the same example.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Four, six, eight still cannot distinguish them.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Three, five, seven separate the predictions.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
Positive testing is not always an ineffective strategy.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Its value depends on context and competing explanations.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Seek information, not automatic opposition.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Write what outcome would change your mind.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Check the quality and source of a counterexample.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
This adaptation does not grade reasoning ability.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Revising a hypothesis is part of thinking.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Seek new clues as well as agreement.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Leave a route for your hypothesis to change.
